### What was ZooKeeper's role in Kafka?

For most of its history, Kafka used ZooKeeper as an external coordination service to store cluster metadata: the list of brokers, topic and partition definitions, which broker leads each partition, configurations, and ACLs. Brokers watched ZooKeeper for changes, and a single broker acted as the controller that pushed metadata updates.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ZooKeeperRole {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ZooKeeper stored (before KRaft):");
        System.out.println("  - Broker registration and health");
        System.out.println("  - Topic definitions and partition count");
        System.out.println("  - Replica assignments per partition");
        System.out.println("  - Leader election state");
        System.out.println("  - Topic configurations");
        System.out.println("  - ACLs (access control lists)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Problems:");
        System.out.println("  - Separate system to deploy, secure, tune, monitor");
        System.out.println("  - Scalability bottleneck for large partition counts");
        System.out.println("  - Slower leader election and metadata propagation");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
ZooKeeper: separate system for metadata, ACLs, leader election
Problems: operational overhead, scalability bottleneck
```

### What is KRaft and how does it work?

KRaft removes ZooKeeper entirely. Metadata now lives in an internal, replicated Kafka log topic named `__cluster_metadata`, and a dedicated set of controller nodes forms a Raft quorum to manage it. Raft is a consensus algorithm where the quorum elects a leader (the active controller) and replicates an ordered log of metadata changes to the follower controllers.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class KRaftArchitecture {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("KRaft Architecture:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Controller Quorum (Raft):");
        System.out.println("  Controller 1: ACTIVE (elected leader)");
        System.out.println("  Controller 2: FOLLOWER");
        System.out.println("  Controller 3: FOLLOWER");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Internal topic: __cluster_metadata");
        System.out.println("  Stores: topics, partitions, replicas, configs, ACLs");
        System.out.println("  Replicated across controller quorum");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Brokers follow the metadata log:");
        System.out.println("  Apply changes incrementally (like a consumer)");
        System.out.println("  No ZooKeeper watching, no full snapshots");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
KRaft: 3-node controller quorum managing __cluster_metadata
Brokers consume metadata changes incrementally
No external ZooKeeper dependency
```

### How is KRaft different from ZooKeeper? (comparison table)

| Feature | ZooKeeper Mode | KRaft Mode |
|---|---|---|
| Metadata storage | External ZooKeeper ensemble | Internal `__cluster_metadata` topic |
| Dependencies | Kafka + ZooKeeper (2 systems) | Kafka only (1 system) |
| Controller election | ZooKeeper ephemeral nodes | Raft consensus |
| Metadata propagation | Watch notifications + snapshots | Incremental log consumption |
| Partition scale | ~200K partition limit | Millions of partitions |
| Controller failover | Slower (ZooKeeper session timeout) | Faster (Raft leader election) |
| Security | Two systems to secure | One system to secure |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ZooKeeperVsKRaft {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ZooKeeper vs KRaft:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ZooKeeper: 2 systems, external dependency,");
        System.out.println("  ~200K partition limit, slower failover");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("KRaft: 1 system, self-contained,");
        System.out.println("  millions of partitions, faster failover");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
ZooKeeper: 2 systems, 200K partition limit, slower failover
KRaft: 1 system, millions of partitions, faster failover
```

### How do you configure KRaft?

In KRaft mode, you configure each node with `process.roles` (broker, controller, or both) and a `controller.quorum.voters` list. Startup uses a formatted cluster id rather than ZooKeeper connection strings.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class KRaftConfiguration {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("KRaft Configuration (server.properties):");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("# Controller-only node:");
        System.out.println("process.roles=controller");
        System.out.println("node.id=1");
        System.out.println("controller.quorum.voters=1@ctrl1:9093,"
            + "2@ctrl2:9093,3@ctrl3:9093");
        System.out.println("controller.listener.names=CONTROLLER");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("# Broker-only node:");
        System.out.println("process.roles=broker");
        System.out.println("node.id=101");
        System.out.println("controller.quorum.voters=1@ctrl1:9093,"
            + "2@ctrl2:9093,3@ctrl3:9093");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("# Combined mode (small cluster):");
        System.out.println("process.roles=broker,controller");
        System.out.println("node.id=1");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Controller-only: process.roles=controller
Broker-only: process.roles=broker
Combined: process.roles=broker,controller (small clusters)
```

### Why does KRaft matter for performance?

Because metadata is itself an event log, brokers consume metadata changes the same way consumers read a topic: they follow the metadata log and apply updates incrementally. This is far more efficient than the old model of watching ZooKeeper and reloading large snapshots. Leader elections and metadata propagation are much faster.

```java
package com.bank.performance;

public class KRaftPerformanceGains {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("KRaft Performance Improvements:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Controller failover:");
        System.out.println("  ZooKeeper: 10-30 seconds (session timeout)");
        System.out.println("  KRaft:     1-3 seconds (Raft election)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Topic creation:");
        System.out.println("  ZooKeeper: write to ZK, notify controller, propagate");
        System.out.println("  KRaft:     controller writes to metadata log, done");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Partition scale:");
        System.out.println("  ZooKeeper: practical limit ~200K partitions");
        System.out.println("  KRaft:     tested to millions of partitions");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("For banking: faster failover = less disruption");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Controller failover: ZK 10-30s -> KRaft 1-3s
Partition scale: ZK ~200K -> KRaft millions
Faster failover = less disruption for banking
```

### What is the migration path from ZooKeeper to KRaft?

KRaft became production-ready in Kafka 3.3, ZooKeeper was deprecated through the 3.x line, and Kafka 4.0 removed ZooKeeper support completely. Teams upgrading from older ZooKeeper-based clusters must perform a migration to KRaft before moving to 4.0.

```java
package com.bank.migration;

public class ZooKeeperToKRaftMigration {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ZooKeeper to KRaft Migration Path:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Kafka 3.3: KRaft production-ready (opt-in)");
        System.out.println("Kafka 3.4-3.9: ZooKeeper deprecated");
        System.out.println("Kafka 4.0: ZooKeeper REMOVED (KRaft only)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Migration Steps:");
        System.out.println("1. Upgrade to Kafka 3.6+ (bridge version)");
        System.out.println("2. Provision controller quorum nodes");
        System.out.println("3. Run migration tool to copy metadata");
        System.out.println("4. Switch brokers to KRaft mode");
        System.out.println("5. Validate in staging");
        System.out.println("6. Decommission ZooKeeper ensemble");
        System.out.println("7. Upgrade to Kafka 4.0");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka 3.3: KRaft ready
Kafka 4.0: ZooKeeper removed
Migrate: provision controllers -> copy metadata -> switch -> validate
```

### What are common mistakes with KRaft?

The most common mistake is trying to upgrade directly to Kafka 4.0 from a ZooKeeper-based cluster without performing the KRaft migration first. Another mistake is sizing the controller quorum with an even number of nodes.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: Even number of controllers.
// 4 controllers need 3 for majority = tolerates only 1 failure.
// 3 controllers need 2 for majority = also tolerates 1 failure.
// 4 controllers adds cost without improving fault tolerance.
public class WrongControllerCount {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: 4 controllers");
        System.out.println("  Majority = 3, tolerates 1 failure");
        System.out.println("  Same as 3 controllers but costs more");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: odd number of controllers");
        System.out.println("  3 controllers: majority=2, tolerates 1 failure");
        System.out.println("  5 controllers: majority=3, tolerates 2 failures");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: even controllers (4) = same fault tolerance as 3 but higher cost
CORRECT: odd controllers (3 or 5) = optimal cost/fault tolerance ratio
```

### What should a senior engineer know about KRaft?

The durability and fault tolerance principles of the controller quorum mirror those of data replication: a quorum of three controllers tolerates one failure, a quorum of five tolerates two. Controller nodes should be on reliable storage and in different availability zones.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class KRaftSeniorInsights {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior KRaft Insights:");
        System.out.println("1. Controller quorum: 3 (prod) or 5 (large/critical)");
        System.out.println("2. Always odd number (Raft majority rule)");
        System.out.println("3. Controllers in different AZs (rack-awareness)");
        System.out.println("4. Reliable storage for metadata log");
        System.out.println("5. Combined mode for dev, separate for production");
        System.out.println("6. Monitor: active controller health");
        System.out.println("7. Monitor: metadata log lag on followers");
        System.out.println("8. Test controller failover regularly");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Senior insights: odd quorum, cross-AZ placement, monitor controller health
```
